    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today with a high-ceiling lineup tailored for a significant offensive output against several vulnerable arms. Our strongest play is undoubtedly **Corbin Carroll** (81.14), who benefits from a prime matchup against Ethan Pecko; the 6.19 xERA presents an ideal environment for **Corbin Carroll** to leverage his elite speed and create havoc on the basepaths. Similarly, **Jose Caballero** (76.79) earns a massive boost due to his dynamic platoon advantage against a left-handed starter, paired with a favorable SP skill profile that allows him to exploit the opposition's weaknesses. **Trevor Story** (75.93) also projects as a tier-one starter today; he draws a rare park-factor tailwind against Chris Bassitt, turning what could be a difficult matchup into a high-leverage scoring opportunity. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) rounds out our primary threats, as he faces Max Scherzer in a matchup where the dynamic platoon splits heavily favor the hitter, effectively neutralizing the veteran pitcher's declining command.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Austin Riley** (43.98), who narrowly missed the cut behind the efficient, high-floor output of our starting infielders. While **Austin Riley** possesses the raw power to change a game, his projected efficiency was hampered by a difficult matchup against Zack Wheeler and a bottom-third batting order placement that severely capped his expected R and RBI production. We opted to prioritize the high-efficiency slots held by **Isaac Paredes** (63.63) and **Gabriel Moreno** (67.84), both of whom benefit significantly from favorable xERA matchups and superior lineup positioning.\n\nOwners should maintain caution regarding the high number of lineups still pending, specifically for **Josh Naylor**, **Max Muncy**, **Cole Young**, **Isaac Paredes**, **James Wood**, and **Jose Caballero**. While our optimizer has accounted for these roles based on historical data, any late-scratch or order-tweak could shift the daily efficiency scores. Furthermore, while **James Wood** (49.75) carries a lower efficiency rating due to the elite presence of Tyler Glasnow on the mound, his internal xwOBA remains a bright spot that keeps him relevant despite the structural risk of facing a high-strikeout arm.",
